saddle to the crupper. ''E. H. Knight.''

'''back-strapped''' (bak′strapt), ''p. a.'' Carried by head-winds to the back of a cape or promontory: said of a ship.

'''back-stream''' (bak′strēm), ''n.'' A current running against the regular course of the stream; an up-stream.

'''back-string''' (bak′string), ''n.'' A leading-string by which a child is supported or guided from behind.
{{smaller block|The ''back-string'' and the bib. {{right|''Cowper'', Task, iv. 228.}}}}

'''back-stroke''' (bak′strōk), ''n.'' '''1.''' A blow or stroke in return.—'''2.''' A backhanded stroke; a back-hander.
{{smaller block|My uncle Toby never took this ''back-stroke'' of my father’s at his hobby-horse kindly.
{{right|''Sterne'', Tristram Shandy, vi. 31.}}}}

'''back-swimmer''' (bak′swim″ėr), ''n.'' Same as ''boat-fly''.

'''back-sword''' (bak′sōrd), ''n.'' '''1.''' A sword with one sharp edge, used for cutting rather than thrusting, sometimes curved, and frequently straight. {{smaller|It usually had a basket-hilt, and was the common weapon of citizens and country people when the rapier and afterward the small-sword were worn by gentlemen.}}
'''2.''' A cudgel fitted with a basket-hilt, used for a particular kind of single-stick play.—'''3.''' A cudgel-play in which the back-sword (in sense 2) is used, peculiar to certain counties of England, and still kept up at festivals and the like in the attempt to preserve old customs. {{smaller|The guard is with the left arm, and the object of each player is to break the skin of his adversary’s forehead so as to draw blood.}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
'''back-tack''' (bak′tak), ''n.'' In ''Scots law'', a tack or lease connected with wadsets or mortgages, by which the possession of the land is returned to the proprietor on payment of a rent corresponding to the interest of the money advanced. See ''wadset.''

'''back-tool''' (bak′töl), ''n.'' Any tool, either fillet or roll, used by bookbinders in decorating the curved surface of the back of a book.

'''back-trick†''' (bak′trik), ''n.'' A caper backward in dancing.
{{smaller block|I have the ''back-trick'' simply as strong as any man in Illyria.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., i. 3.}}}}

'''backward, backwards''' (bak′wärd, -wärdz), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''bakward, bacward'', adv., by apheresis for ''abackward'', < ''abak'', adv., back, + ''-ward, -wards.''] '''1.''' In the direction of the back: as, to throw the arms ''backward.''—'''2.''' With the back first in the direction of motion: as, to walk ''backward;'' to fall ''backward.''
{{smaller block|He [Eli] fell from off the seat ''backward'', . . . and his neck brake.
{{right|1 Sam. iv. 18.}}
Thou wilt fall ''backward.'' {{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., i. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' In the direction from which one has come; toward that which is or has been left behind: as, he glanced ''backward.''—'''4.''' Toward bygone times or events; toward that which is past in time: as, to look ''backward'' to the last century.
{{smaller block|The lights of memory ''backward'' stream.
{{right|''Whittier'', Memories.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''5.''' In or by reflection; reflexively.
{{smaller block|<poem>The mind can ''backward'' cast
Upon herself her understanding light.</poem>
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Introd. to Immortal. of Soul.}}}}

'''6.''' In time past; ago.
{{smaller block|Some reigns ''backward.''
{{right|''Locke.''}}}}

'''7.''' In an opposite or contrary direction.
{{smaller block|For every two steps they made forwards and upwards they slipped one ''backwards.''
{{right|''Lady Brassey'', Voyage of Sunbeam, I. ii.}}}}

'''8.''' In an opposite or reverse order; from the end toward the beginning; in an order contrary to the natural order: as, to read or spell ''backward;'' hence, perversely; in a wrong or perverse manner.
{{smaller block|<poem>I never yet saw man,
How wise, how noble, young, how rarely featur’d,
But she would spell him ''backward.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, iii. 1.}}
The gospel of Christ is read ''backwards'', when that world which he came to save is regarded as a world which it is a merit to abandon.
{{right|''C. E. Norton'', Travel and Study in Italy, p. 47.}}}}

'''9.''' From a better to a worse state; retrogressively.
{{smaller block|<poem>The work went ''backward'' ; and the more he strove
T’ advance the suit, the farther from her love. ''Dryden.''</poem>}}

{{smaller|'''Backward and forward''', to and fro.—'''To ring bells backward''', to give an alarm by ringing the bells of a chime in the wrong order, beginning with the bass bell.}}
{{smaller block|The ''bells'' they ''ring backward'', the drums they are beat.
{{right|''Scott'', Bonnie Dundee.}}}}

'''backward''' (back′wärd), ''a.'' [< ''backward, adv.''] '''1.''' Directed to the back or rear: as, “a ''backward'' look,” ''Shak.'', Sonnets, lix.—'''2.''' Reversed;

readings.c2s2:
returning; directed to or toward the original starting-point: as, a ''backward'' movement or journey.
{{smaller block|<poem>And now they do re-stem
Their ''backward'' course.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Othello, i. 3.}}}}
'''3.''' Done in reverse order; done in an order contrary to the natural order, as in repeating a sentence from the end to the beginning.
{{smaller block|<poem>Without his rod reversed,
And ''backward'' mutters of dissevering power,
We cannot free the lady.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 817.}}}}
'''4.''' Being in, or placed at, the back.
{{smaller block|Four legs and two voices. . . . His forward voice now is to speak well of his friend; his ''backward'' voice is to utter foul speeches, and to detract.
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, ii. 2.}}}}
'''5.''' Slow; sluggish; unprogressive; unadvanced; behind in progress: as, a ''backward'' learner.
{{smaller block|Brigandage survives only in out-of-the-way corners of the most ''backward'' countries of Christendom, such as Spain and Sicily.
{{right|''J. Fiske'', Evolutionist, p. 229.}}}}
'''6.''' Late; behind in time; coming after something else, or after the usual time: as, ''backward'' fruits; the season is ''backward.''
{{smaller block|A dry, cold, ''backward'' spring, easterly winds.
{{right|''Evelyn'', Diary, April 15, 1688.}}}}
'''7.''' Holding back; averse; reluctant; hesitating.
{{smaller block|The mind is ''backward'' to undergo the fatigue of weighing every argument.
{{right|''Watts.''}}
<poem>For wiser brutes were ''backward'' to be slaves.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Windsor Forest, l. 50.}}}}
'''8.''' Timid; bashful; retiring in disposition; modest.—'''9.''' Reaching back into the past;

readings.c2s3:
already past.
{{smaller block|Flies unconscious o’er each ''backward'' year.
{{right|''Byron'', Childe Harold, ii. 24.}}}}

'''backward†''' (bak′wärd), ''n.'' [< ''backward, a.''] The things or state behind or past.
{{smaller block|<poem>What see’st thou else
In the dark ''backward'' and abysm of time?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, i. 2.}}}}

'''backward†''' (bak′wärd), ''v. t.'' [< ''backward, adv.''] To obstruct; keep back; retard; delay.
{{smaller block|Doth clog and ''backward'' us. ''Hammond'', Sermons, xv.}}

'''backwardation''' (bak-wär-dā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''backward, v.'', + ''-ation.''] On the London Stock Exchange, the premium paid by a seller of stock for the privilege of postponing its delivery to the buyer until the next fortnightly settling-day. See ''contango.''

'''backwardly''' (bak′wärd-li), ''adv.'' '''1.''' In a backward direction.
{{smaller block|The mandible is extremely massive and has a ''backwardly'' produced angle. {{right|''Huxley'', Anat. Vert., p. 320.}}}}
'''2.''' Unwillingly; reluctantly; aversely; perversely; ill.
{{smaller block|<poem>I was the first man
That e’er receiv’d gift from him;
And does he think so ''backwardly'' of me now,
That I’ll requite it last?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of A., iii. 3.}}}}

'''backwardness''' (bak′wärd-nes), ''n.'' The state or quality of being backward. {{smaller|(''a'') Backward state as regards progress; slowness; tardiness: as, the ''back-''}}

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller|''wardness'' of the spring. (''b'') Unwillingness; reluctance: dilatoriness or dullness in action.}}
{{smaller block|Our ''backwardness'' to good works.
{{right|''Bp. Atterbury.''}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Bashfulness; shyness.}}

'''backwards''', ''adv.'' See ''backward.''

'''back-washed''' (bak′wosht), ''a.'' Cleansed from oil, as wool after combing.

'''back-water''' (bak′wâ″tėr), ''n.'' '''1†.''' Water flowing in from behind.—'''2.''' Water thrown back by the turning of a water-wheel or the paddles of steamboats, etc.—'''3.''' Water held or forced back, as in a mill-race or in a tributary stream, in consequence of some obstruction, as a dam or flood.—'''4.''' An artificial accumulation of water obtained at high tide and reserved in reservoirs, to be discharged at low tide for clearing off deposits in channel-beds and tideways.—'''5.''' A creek or arm of the sea which runs parallel to the coast, having only a narrow slip of land between it and the sea, and communicating with the latter by barred entrances.
{{smaller block|Entering the mouth of the Moredab, an extensive ''back-water'' into which fall the Piri-Bazaar and other streams, we come alongside a fairly constructed quay.
{{right|''O’Donovan'', Merv, viii.}}}}

'''backwood''' (bak′wu̇d), ''n.'' That portion of a carpenter’s plane which is immediately behind the plane-iron.—'''To drive the backwood up''', {{smaller|to drive the wedge of a plane too tightly. When this is done the pressure of the plane-iron raises a bur or slight ridge at the angle of the mouth and sole.}}

